  "notes": "West leads the ♣Q against 5♥. West's club lead goes to the ♣A and East returns the suit, South winning the ♣K. South draws 2 rounds of trump. West discards on the second round.\n\nWest has shown at least 5 cards in spades and a 5 card minor (presumably clubs) with his Michael's cue bid. South will not be able to ruff both of his club losers in dummy as East will be able to overruff.\n\nSouth may be able to put the West hand under pressure. West must be 5125. He must hold the ♦K for the contract to have a chance. The bidding and early defense suggest the ♠A and ♠K are split.\n\nSouth can draw the last trump and take a diamond finesse. The ♦A is cashed, dropping the ♦K. Another diamond trick is cashed. The ♦5 is ruffed by South. On this trick West is forced to come down to 2 spades and 2 clubs.\n\nSouth now leads the ♠2. If West wins the trick, he will be endplayed, forced to concede a trick in either spades or clubs. If West plays low, an honour is played from North which East wins. East has only spades left and must play one. South ruffs and West's spade honour will show up. The dummy will be high.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♣Q against 5♥. West's club lead goes to the ♣A and East returns the suit, South winning the ♣K. South draws 2 rounds of trump. West discards on the second round.",
    "West has shown at least 5 cards in spades and a 5 card minor (presumably clubs) with his Michael's cue bid. South will not be able to ruff both of his club losers in dummy as East will be able to overruff.",
    "South may be able to put the West hand under pressure. West must be 5125. He must hold the ♦K for the contract to have a chance. The bidding and early defense suggest the ♠A and ♠K are split.",
    "South can draw the last trump and take a diamond finesse. The ♦A is cashed, dropping the ♦K. Another diamond trick is cashed. The ♦5 is ruffed by South. On this trick West is forced to come down to 2 spades and 2 clubs.",
    "South now leads the ♠2. If West wins the trick, he will be endplayed, forced to concede a trick in either spades or clubs. If West plays low, an honour is played from North which East wins. East has only spades left and must play one. South ruffs and West's spade honour will show up. The dummy will be high."
